    widths=[1.0, 0.55, 0.65, 0.65, 0.65], fontsize=7.5,
)

subheading("D", "Why These Subset Sizes")
para(
    "Using 10,000 training and 2,000 validation images rather than the full 60,000-image Fashion-MNIST "
    "training set is a deliberate budget decision, not a limitation of the method. Pilot runs showed that "
    "validation MSE improves only marginally past 10,000 images for these small architectures\u2014the "
    "bottleneck, not the data, is the binding constraint\u2014while training time grows linearly. The "
    "subset also keeps the full five-model study, including ablations and figure generation, inside a "
    "ten-minute CPU budget, which matters for reproducibility: any reviewer or student can rerun the entire "
    "pipeline on a laptop. The test set, by contrast, is kept at the full 10,000 images, because detection "
    "metrics\u2014especially precision at low thresholds\u2014need large samples to be stable. Data is "
    "spent where it reduces variance (evaluation) and saved where it does not (training)."
)

subheading("E", "Computational Profile and Software Environment")
para(
    "Table VII records the software stack so the results can be reproduced exactly; every run executes on CPU. "
    "Table VIII compares the computational cost of the three models. Two observations are worth making "
    "explicit. First, the Conv-AE uses 4.2\u00d7 fewer parameters than the FC-AE but performs 7.4\u00d7 more "
    "multiply-accumulate operations per forward pass (7.98 vs. 1.08 million): convolutions reuse each weight "
    "at every spatial position, so parameter count and FLOPs measure genuinely different things, and a "
    "\"smaller\" model can still be the more compute-hungry one. Second, measured single-image inference on a "
    "two-core CPU is 0.03 ms for the FC-AE and 0.21 ms for the Conv-AE (batch size 64, evaluation mode)\u2014"
    "thousands of images per second with no accelerator, a fact we return to in Section IX. The Denoising-AE "
    "shares the Conv-AE backbone, so its inference cost is identical; the noise corruption applies at "
    "training time only."
)
add_table(
    ["Component", "Version"],
    [
        ["Python", "3.12.3"],
        ["PyTorch", "2.14.0+cpu"],
        ["torchvision", "0.29.0+cpu"],
        ["NumPy", "2.5.2"],
        ["scikit-learn", "1.9.1"],
        ["Matplotlib", "3.11.2"],
    ],
    "TABLE VII", "Software environment (all runs on CPU).",
    widths=[1.5, 1.5], fontsize=8,
)
add_table(
    ["Model", "Parameters", "MACs / forward", "Inference (CPU)", "Epochs"],
    [
        ["FC-AE", "1,086,768", "1.08 M", "0.03 ms / image", "8"],
        ["Conv-AE", "256,257", "7.98 M", "0.21 ms / image", "8"],
        ["Denoising-AE", "256,257 (shared)", "7.98 M", "0.21 ms / image", "8"],
    ],
    "TABLE VIII", "Computational profile. Inference timed on a two-core CPU, batch 64, eval mode.",
    widths=[0.95, 0.75, 0.75, 0.85, 0.55], fontsize=7.5,
)

subheading("F", "Fixed Design Choices and Their Rationale")
para(
    "Several hyperparameters were fixed by judgment rather than search, and Table XIII records each choice "
    "with its rationale so that future work knows exactly what to vary. The latent dimension d = 32 "
    "compresses 784 pixels by a factor of 24.5, narrow enough to force genuine abstraction but wide enough "
    "to reconstruct garment detail; it follows the convention of the small-autoencoder literature rather "
    "than any tuning on our part. The denoising noise level \u03c3 = 0.3 was chosen to destroy fine texture "
    "while preserving silhouettes\u2014at \u03c3 = 0.1 the corruption would be nearly invisible, and at "
    "\u03c3 = 0.5 even the garment outline would dissolve, leaving nothing to learn from. Dropout at 0.1 "
    "regularizes lightly without starving the small networks of capacity. The 95th-percentile threshold fixes "
    "the operating point before any anomaly is seen, as Section II requires. Eight epochs suffice because "
    "pilot runs showed validation loss flattening by epoch 6 (see Appendix A); training longer would spend "
    "compute for diminishing returns on this subset."
)
add_table(
    ["Choice", "Value", "Rationale"],
    [
        ["Latent dimension d", "32", "24.5\u00d7 compression; literature convention"],
        ["Denoising noise \u03c3", "0.3", "Destroys texture, preserves silhouette; fixed a priori"],
        ["Dropout rate", "0.1", "Light regularization for small networks"],
        ["Threshold \u03c4", "95th pct. of normal val. errors", "Fixes 5% FPR without seeing anomalies"],
        ["Epochs", "8 (baselines) / 4 (ablations)", "Val. loss flat by epoch 6 in pilots"],
        ["Random seed", "42", "Single fixed seed; init, shuffle, noise"],
    ],
    "TABLE XIII", "Design choices fixed without tuning.",
    widths=[1.0, 1.05, 1.95], fontsize=7.5,
)

subheading("G", "Preprocessing Pipeline")
para(
    "Preprocessing is deliberately minimal, and the minimalism is a choice. Images enter the pipeline as "
    "28\u00d728 uint8 arrays and are converted to float32 tensors scaled to [0, 1] by division by 255\u2014"
    "the only transformation applied. No mean subtraction, no whitening, no data augmentation, and no "
    "histogram equalization are used. This keeps the pixel semantics intact: the sigmoid output layer "
    "produces values in [0, 1] matched to the input range, so the MSE in (2) compares like with like. "
    "Whitening, as advocated by Coates et al. [29] for single-layer feature learning, would decorrelate "
    "pixels and arguably improve the codes, but it would also move the anomaly score away from raw visual "
    "difference, complicating the operator-facing interpretation developed in Section IX. Augmentation "
    "(rotations, crops) is standard in supervised training but would blur the one-class protocol: an "
    "augmented sandal is still a sandal, and teaching the model invariance to geometric transforms could "
    "only dilute the anomaly signal. The pipeline is therefore: load, scale, split\u2014nothing more."
)

# ================================================================ VI. RESULTS
heading("VI", "Results")

para(
    "Table II summarizes the three architectures. Table IX reports the main results: final test reconstruction "
    "MSE on normal images, the 95th-percentile decision threshold, and the five anomaly detection metrics. "
    "Every number below comes from a single fixed-seed run under the protocol of Section IV."
)
add_table(
    ["Model", "Encoder", "Latent", "Decoder", "Params"],
    [
        ["FC-AE", "784\u2192512\u2192256\u219232\n(BN+ReLU+Dropout 0.1)", "32",
         "32\u2192256\u2192512\u2192784\n(sigmoid out)", "1,086,768"],
        ["Conv-AE", "Conv 1\u219232\u219264\n(stride 2, BN+ReLU)", "32",
         "Transposed conv\n64\u219232\u21921 (sigmoid out)", "256,257"],
        ["Denoising-AE", "Conv backbone\n+ Gaussian noise \u03c3=0.3", "32",
         "Transposed conv\n64\u219232\u21921 (sigmoid out)", "256,257"],
    ],
    "TABLE II", "Architecture and parameter summary.",
    widths=[0.75, 1.05, 0.40, 1.05, 0.75], fontsize=7.5,
)
add_table(
    ["Model", "MSE", "Threshold", "Precision", "Recall", "F1", "ROC-AUC", "PR-AUC"],
    [
        ["FC-AE", "0.01979", "0.03901", "0.401", "0.301", "0.344", "0.843", "0.370"],
        ["Conv-AE", "0.01459", "0.02882", "0.416", "0.321", "0.363", "0.857", "0.368"],
        ["Denoising-AE", "0.01668", "0.03215", "0.404", "0.305", "0.348", "0.843", "0.355"],
    ],
    "TABLE IX", "One-class anomaly detection results (Sandal held out; threshold = 95th percentile of normal errors).",
    widths=[0.85, 0.50, 0.55, 0.50, 0.40, 0.35, 0.50, 0.50], fontsize=7.5,
)
para(
    "Three patterns stand out. First, the convolutional autoencoder wins on every reconstruction-related "
    "measure: its test MSE of 0.01459 is 26% lower than the fully connected baseline\u2019s 0.01979, despite using "
    "roughly four times fewer parameters. Weight sharing is doing real work here\u2014local features such as "
    "collar edges, sleeve seams, and fabric texture recur across image positions, and the convolutional encoder "
    "exploits that regularity instead of relearning it at every pixel location the way dense layers must. The "
    "denoising model lands between the two on clean-image MSE (0.01668): it pays a small price on uncorrupted "
    "inputs because part of its capacity is devoted to noise removal rather than pixel-perfect copying, exactly "
    "the trade-off the denoising objective is designed to impose."
)
add_figure("loss_curves.png",
           "Training and validation MSE loss curves for the three autoencoder variants over 8 epochs. "
           "All models converge smoothly with no sign of overfitting; the convolutional models settle at "
           "substantially lower loss than the fully connected baseline.")
para(
    "Second, reconstruction quality translates into detection quality but not one-to-one. The Conv-AE achieves "
    "the best ROC-AUC, 0.857, meaning that a randomly chosen Sandal scores higher than a randomly chosen normal "
    "image about 86% of the time\u2014genuine separability, well above chance. The FC-AE and Denoising-AE tie at "
    "0.843, a small but consistent gap behind. PR-AUC tells a subtler story: all three models sit near "
    "0.36\u20130.37, far above the 0.10 random baseline for a 10%-prevalence task but modest in absolute terms, "
    "reflecting how hard it is to rank every anomaly above every normal image when the two distributions "
    "overlap. Notably, the FC-AE edges out the Conv-AE on PR-AUC (0.370 vs. 0.368) even while losing on ROC-AUC, "
    "a reminder that the two metrics weight different regions of the ranking and that no single number tells "
    "the whole story."
)
add_figure("recon_fc.png",
           "Original images (top row) versus reconstructions (bottom row) from the fully connected "
           "autoencoder. Global shape is preserved but fine texture is visibly smoothed.")
add_figure("recon_conv.png",
           "Original images (top row) versus reconstructions (bottom row) from the convolutional "
           "autoencoder. Edges and fabric detail survive noticeably better than in Fig. 2.")
add_figure("recon_den.png",
           "Denoising autoencoder: clean inputs (top), inputs corrupted with Gaussian noise \u03c3=0.3 "
           "(middle), and the model\u2019s reconstructions (bottom), which recover the clean image.")
para(
    "Third, the threshold-dependent metrics are modest across the board\u2014precision around 0.40, recall "
    "around 0.31, F1 near 0.35\u2014and this deserves explanation rather than alarm. The 95th-percentile rule is "
    "a deliberately conservative operating point: by construction it flags 5% of normal images, so with 9,000 "
    "normal and 1,000 anomaly test images, even a perfect ranker would report precision of only "
    "1,000/(1,000+450) \u2248 0.69 at full recall. Our models additionally miss roughly two thirds of Sandals, "
    "because many sandals\u2014particularly dark, low-contrast ones\u2014reconstruct almost as well as normal "
    "footwear. The error histograms in Fig. 5 make this overlap visible: the anomaly distribution is shifted "
    "right, but its left tail extends deep into the normal range. The ROC curves in Fig. 6 confirm that the "
    "separation is real but partial, exactly what an ROC-AUC of 0.857 implies."
)
para(
    "The denoising results deserve a closer look because they illustrate what the extra MSE point actually buys. "
    "In Fig. 4, inputs corrupted with noise at \u03c3=0.3 are barely recognizable\u2014thin straps vanish into "
    "static and dark regions bloom with speckles\u2014yet the model\u2019s reconstructions recover clean silhouettes "
    "and plausible fabric shading. The network has learned a prior over garment appearance strong enough to "
    "inpaint through heavy corruption, which is precisely the behavior one wants when sensor noise or "
    "compression artifacts contaminate inspection imagery. That this robustness costs only 0.002 in clean-image "
    "MSE relative to the plain Conv-AE (0.01668 vs. 0.01459) suggests the denoising objective acts as a cheap, "
    "well-targeted regularizer rather than a competing task."
)
para(
    "Failure analysis rounds out the picture. The Sandals the models miss most consistently share two traits: "
    "low overall contrast, where the dark shoe blends into the black background, and unusually plain "
    "construction, such as flat flip-flop-like profiles without straps. Both traits make the image easy to "
    "reconstruct from a generic footwear prior, so the anomaly score stays low. Conversely, the normal images "
    "most often flagged are high-contrast outliers within their own classes\u2014a white sneaker on the dark "
    "background, a bag with an unusual buckle\u2014whose pixel statistics genuinely deviate from the class "
    "typical. These are not modeling failures so much as consequences of scoring by pixel reconstruction: the "
    "metric is honest about what it measures, and its blind spots are interpretable rather than mysterious."
)
para(
    "It is equally important to state what the numbers rule out, because negative results discipline future "
    "work. First, parameter count does not explain performance: the Conv-AE has roughly one-quarter the "
    "parameters of the FC-AE yet wins on every metric, so the naive \u201cbigger is better\u201d hypothesis is "
    "rejected and the credit belongs to the convolutional structure. Second, the denoising objective does "
    "not improve anomaly detection here despite its theoretical appeal\u2014its ROC-AUC (0.843) merely ties "
    "the FC-AE and trails the plain Conv-AE (0.857), so robustness to synthetic noise does not transfer to "
    "robustness against semantic anomalies at \u03c3 = 0.3. Third, no model approaches the precision needed "
    "for fully automatic rejection: even the best operating point in Table XIV leaves six of ten flags as "
    "false alarms, which rules out any deployment story without a human in the loop. These are the "
    "boundaries within which the positive findings\u2014convolutions win, batch normalization is "
    "load-bearing, rankings are threshold-invariant\u2014should be read."
)
add_figure("error_hist.png",
           "Distribution of per-image reconstruction errors for normal test images versus Sandal anomalies "
           "(Conv-AE). The dashed line marks the 95th-percentile threshold; the overlapping tails explain "
           "the modest precision and recall at this operating point.")
add_figure("roc_pr.png",
           "ROC curves (left) and precision\u2013recall curves (right) for the three models. The Conv-AE "
           "dominates the ROC plot; all three clearly exceed the 0.10 PR random baseline.")
para(
    "The latent-space projections in Fig. 7 provide a final sanity check. Both PCA and t-SNE (perplexity 30) "
    "embeddings of the Conv-AE codes show coherent class clusters\u2014trousers apart from bags, tops near "
    "shirts\u2014and the held-out Sandal points form a distinct cloud at the edge of the normal manifold. The "
    "encoder therefore organizes unseen anomalies sensibly without ever having been trained on them, which is "
    "precisely the behavior a one-class system requires. Figure 8 grounds the numbers in examples: the "
    "highest-scoring images are unmistakably sandals with thin straps and open toes, while the lowest-scoring "
    "images are plain, high-contrast garments the model reconstructs almost perfectly."
)
add_figure("latent_conv.png",
           "PCA (left) and t-SNE with perplexity 30 (right) projections of the 32-dimensional Conv-AE latent "
           "codes, colored by true class. Held-out Sandals cluster at the edge of the normal manifold.")
add_figure("anomaly_conv.png",
           "Conv-AE anomaly scores: highest-scoring test images (top), dominated by strappy sandals, versus "
           "lowest-scoring images (bottom), plain garments reconstructed almost perfectly.")

subheading("G", "Per-Class Reconstruction Analysis")
para(
    "Aggregate MSE hides which garments are easy and which are hard. Table XI breaks the test reconstruction "
    "error down by true class for all three models, and three findings emerge. First, the ranking of class "
    "difficulty is essentially architecture-independent: Trouser is the easiest class for every model "
    "(Conv-AE MSE 0.00966) and Bag the hardest normal class (FC-AE MSE 0.02976), with Ankle boot second "
    "hardest. The Spearman rank correlation of the per-class orderings is 0.95 between the FC-AE and the "
    "Conv-AE, 0.95 between the FC-AE and the Denoising-AE, and 1.00 between the two convolutional models. "
    "Difficulty is therefore a property of the data, not of the architecture: trousers are simple elongated "
    "shapes with little texture variance and compress almost trivially, while bags carry handles, buckles, "
    "and clasps whose positions vary wildly, defeating any compact code."
)
para(
    "Second, the held-out Sandal class is harder than every normal class for every model\u2014its mean "
    "error (0.03578 FC, 0.02694 Conv, 0.02940 Denoising) exceeds even Bag, the hardest normal class. This is "
    "the quantitative counterpart of the latent-space finding in Section VIII: the anomaly distribution is "
    "shifted right as a whole, not merely in its tail, which is why ranking metrics like ROC-AUC are strong "
    "even though the fixed-threshold precision is modest. Third, the denoising model sits between the other "
    "two on every single class, never best and never worst\u2014consistent with the interpretation that the "
    "denoising objective trades a small uniform reconstruction penalty for robustness, rather than "
    "specializing in any particular garment type."
)
para(
    "This analysis also explains two qualitative observations from Section VI. The false positives\u2014"
    "normal images flagged as anomalies\u2014are disproportionately Bags and Ankle boots, the classes whose "
    "normal error distributions already reach into the threshold region; they are hard for the model even "
    "when they are not anomalies. Conversely, the missed Sandals (false negatives) are the ones whose error "
    "falls in the Bag-like range of normal variation. A class-conditional threshold\u2014one \u03c4 per "
    "predicted class\u2014would exploit this structure, and we note it as a concrete, low-cost improvement "
    "for future work."
)
add_table(
    ["Class", "FC-AE", "Conv-AE", "Denoising-AE"],
    [
        ["T-shirt/top", "0.01905", "0.01344", "0.01575"],
        ["Trouser", "0.01354", "0.00966", "0.01165"],
        ["Pullover", "0.01953", "0.01526", "0.01735"],
        ["Dress", "0.01842", "0.01355", "0.01577"],
        ["Coat", "0.01850", "0.01362", "0.01582"],
        ["Shirt", "0.01920", "0.01485", "0.01689"],
        ["Sneaker", "0.01731", "0.01293", "0.01440"],
        ["Bag", "0.02976", "0.02225", "0.02477"],
        ["Ankle boot", "0.02280", "0.01576", "0.01775"],
        ["Sandal (anomaly)", "0.03578", "0.02694", "0.02940"],
    ],
    "TABLE XI", "Per-class mean test reconstruction MSE (1,000 images per class).",
    widths=[1.0, 0.65, 0.65, 0.75], fontsize=7.5,
)

subheading("H", "Operating-Point Analysis")
para(
    "The 95th-percentile threshold used so far is principled but arbitrary, so Table XIV sweeps the "
    "operating point across the 90th, 95th, and 99th percentiles of normal validation error for all three "
    "models. The trade-off is stark and instructive. Relaxing the threshold to the 90th percentile raises "
    "recall substantially\u2014from 0.321 to 0.481 for the Conv-AE\u2014at the cost of precision, and it "
    "is at this looser setting that F1 is actually maximized for every model (0.404 Conv, 0.393 Denoising, "
    "0.373 FC). Tightening to the 99th percentile flips the balance: precision climbs to 0.54\u20130.60, "
    "but recall collapses to roughly 0.10\u20130.14 and F1 falls below 0.22 across the board."
)
para(
    "Two lessons follow. First, the 95th-percentile rule we adopted is conservative: it prioritizes a low "
    "false-alarm rate over catching anomalies, which suits an operator-triage setting but leaves F1 on the "
    "table. Had we tuned the threshold on a held-out anomaly sample\u2014something the honest one-class "
    "protocol forbids\u2014the 90th percentile would have been the better choice. Second, and more "
    "importantly, the ranking of the three models is invariant across all three operating points: Conv-AE "
    "leads on F1 at every threshold, the Denoising-AE is second, and the FC-AE third. The architectural "
    "conclusion of Section VI therefore does not depend on where \u03c4 is drawn, which is exactly what the "
    "threshold-free ROC-AUC already suggested. For deployment (Section IX), this table\u2014not a single "
    "F1 number\u2014is the artifact that matters: it lets the operating point be chosen from the economics "
    "of false alarms versus missed defects."
)
add_table(
    ["Model", "Percentile", "Threshold", "Precision", "Recall", "F1"],
    [
        ["FC-AE", "90th", "0.03245", "0.326", "0.435", "0.373"],
        ["", "95th", "0.03901", "0.401", "0.301", "0.344"],
        ["", "99th", "0.05417", "0.600", "0.135", "0.220"],
        ["Conv-AE", "90th", "0.02410", "0.348", "0.481", "0.404"],
        ["", "95th", "0.02882", "0.416", "0.321", "0.363"],
        ["", "99th", "0.04191", "0.536", "0.104", "0.174"],
        ["Denoising-AE", "90th", "0.02693", "0.341", "0.465", "0.393"],
        ["", "95th", "0.03215", "0.404", "0.305", "0.348"],
        ["", "99th", "0.04562", "0.541", "0.106", "0.177"],
    ],
    "TABLE XIV", "Operating-point sweep: metrics at three threshold percentiles.",
    widths=[0.85, 0.60, 0.60, 0.60, 0.50, 0.45], fontsize=7.5,
)

# ================================================================ VIII. LATENT ANALYSIS
heading("VIII", "Latent-Space and Qualitative Analysis")

para(
    "Section VI established that the models separate Sandals from normal images; this section asks what the "
    "encoders actually learned in order to do so. Figures 7, 9, and 10 project the 32-dimensional codes of the "
    "three models with PCA and t-SNE (perplexity 30), colored by true class. For the FC-AE (Fig. 9), the PCA "
    "view shows Sandal points (brown) as a dense blob on the right side of the cloud, already well separated "
    "from most normal classes along the first principal component. The t-SNE view sharpens the picture: "
    "Sandals form a large coherent cluster on the right, adjacent to Sneaker (gray) and Ankle boot (cyan) but "
    "clearly distinct, while Trouser (orange) collapses into a tight isolated island at the top and Bag "
    "(olive-green) forms a small compact group. The Denoising-AE (Fig. 10) tells a similar story with its own "
    "geometry: PCA places Sandals to the right and pushes Trousers downward into a separate lobe, and t-SNE "
    "again isolates Sandals at the top right. The Conv-AE (Fig. 7) shows the same qualitative structure with "
    "slightly cleaner class boundaries, consistent with its stronger quantitative results."
)
add_figure("latent_fc.png",
           "PCA (left) and t-SNE with perplexity 30 (right) projections of the FC-AE latent codes, colored by "
           "true class. Sandal points (brown) form a dense cluster on the right, adjacent to other footwear.")
add_figure("latent_den.png",
           "PCA (left) and t-SNE with perplexity 30 (right) projections of the Denoising-AE latent codes. "
           "Sandals cluster at the top right; trousers (orange) are displaced into a separate lobe.")
para(
    "Two regularities hold across all three encoders. First, the footwear classes\u2014Sandal, Sneaker, Ankle "
    "boot\u2014occupy neighboring regions of the latent space, while garments with wholly different "
    "silhouettes (Trouser, Bag) sit far away. The encoders discover this semantic grouping without any class "
    "labels, purely from the pressure to reconstruct. Second, the held-out Sandal class lands coherently, "
    "adjacent to its semantic neighbors, rather than scattering across the map. That is exactly the behavior "
    "a one-class system needs: the model has learned a structured notion of footwear normality, and Sandals "
    "violate it in a consistent direction, which is why a simple error threshold can catch them."
)
para(
    "Figures 8, 11, and 12 ground these abstractions in examples. For the FC-AE (Fig. 11), the eight "
    "highest-error Sandals (MSE from 0.0557 down to 0.0151) are high-contrast, strappy styles, and their "
    "reconstructions collapse into blurry generic blobs\u2014the network renders what a typical shoe looks "
    "like rather than the input it was given. The Denoising-AE (Fig. 12, MSE from 0.0462 down to 0.0121) "
    "fails on the same strappy styles, though its reconstructions carry a noisier texture, a residue of its "
    "noise-robustness training. The top FC-AE anomaly (MSE 0.0557) exceeds the 95th-percentile threshold "
    "(0.03901) by a wide margin and is comfortably flagged, while the eighth (0.0151) sits well below it and "
    "would be missed\u2014a concrete illustration of why recall at this operating point is only about 0.30."
)
add_figure("anomaly_fc.png",
           "FC-AE: eight highest-error Sandal anomalies (top row, MSE 0.0557 down to 0.0151) and their "
           "reconstructions (bottom row), which collapse to blurry generic blobs.")
add_figure("anomaly_den.png",
           "Denoising-AE: eight highest-error Sandal anomalies (top row, MSE 0.0462 down to 0.0121) and "
           "their reconstructions (bottom row).")
para(
    "The qualitative conclusion is that reconstruction failure is interpretable, not mysterious. Thin straps, "
    "open toes, and high contrast defeat the learned normal prior\u2014precisely the visual attributes that "
    "distinguish sandals from closed footwear. A deployed system can exploit this: showing the operator the "
    "input alongside its reconstruction (as in Figs. 8, 11, and 12) turns the anomaly score into an "
    "explanation, which we develop further in Section IX."
)

# ================================================================ VII. ABLATION
heading("VII", "Ablation Study")
para(
    "To understand which components of the baseline actually matter, we trained two variants of the FC-AE for "
    "4 epochs each: one with batch normalization removed (fc_no_bn) and one with dropout removed (fc_no_drop), "
    "keeping everything else identical. Table X compares them against the full baseline."
)
add_table(
    ["Variant", "MSE", "ROC-AUC", "PR-AUC"],
    [
        ["fc_no_bn", "0.03767", "0.773", "0.253"],
        ["fc_no_drop", "0.02144", "0.817", "0.339"],
        ["fc_base (full)", "0.01979", "0.843", "0.370"],
    ],
    "TABLE X", "Ablation of regularization components (4-epoch runs; baseline shown for reference).",
    widths=[1.0, 0.65, 0.65, 0.65], fontsize=8,
)
para(
    "Batch normalization is critical. Removing it nearly doubles reconstruction error, from 0.01979 to 0.03767, "
    "and collapses detection performance: ROC-AUC falls from 0.843 to 0.773 and PR-AUC from 0.370 to 0.253. The "
    "mechanism is visible in the training logs\u2014without normalization, the deep stack of dense layers suffers "
    "unstable gradient magnitudes across layers, activations drift as training proceeds, and the network "
    "settles into a markedly worse local optimum within the same epoch budget. In practical terms, batch "
    "normalization is not a minor tweak here but the difference between a working model and a broken one."
)
para(
    "Dropout matters less but still helps. Removing it raises MSE modestly from 0.01979 to 0.02144 and costs "
    "about 0.026 ROC-AUC points (0.843 to 0.817) and 0.031 PR-AUC points. Dropout\u2019s role is regularization "
    "rather than optimization: by randomly deactivating 10% of hidden units during training, it prevents the "
    "network from leaning on brittle co-adapted feature combinations and forces redundant, distributed "
    "representations of garment structure. Those redundant representations generalize better to unseen normal "
    "variation\u2014wrinkles, folds, and lighting shifts\u2014which is exactly what the validation-error threshold "
    "rewards. The effect size is small because 4\u20138 epochs on 10,000 images is a light-training regime where "
    "overfitting is mild to begin with; we expect dropout\u2019s contribution to grow with longer training."
)
para(
    "Why does batch normalization matter so much in this setting? The fully connected autoencoder is a deep, "
    "narrow network: six nonlinear layers compress 784 inputs through a 32-dimensional bottleneck, and "
    "without normalization the activation statistics drift from layer to layer as training proceeds\u2014the "
    "internal covariate shift that Ioffe and Szegedy [22] identified. In a bottleneck architecture the "
    "damage compounds: the encoder\u2019s later layers receive shifting inputs, so the code z becomes a "
    "moving target, and the decoder must then invert an unstable representation. The sigmoid output layer "
    "amplifies the problem, because saturated sigmoids kill gradients for any unit whose pre-activation "
    "drifts too far. Batch normalization pins each layer\u2019s activations to zero mean and unit variance, "
    "keeping sigmoids in their responsive regime and giving the bottleneck a stable coordinate system to "
    "learn in. Four epochs are not enough for the unnormalized network to recover that stability on its "
    "own\u2014hence the near-doubling of MSE. The lesson generalizes beyond our study: in deep narrow "
    "autoencoders, normalization is not a training convenience but a structural requirement."
)
para(
    "Taken together, the ablations justify the baseline design: batch normalization earns its place through "
    "optimization stability, dropout through mild but consistent generalization gains, and neither can be "
    "dropped without measurable cost."
)

# ================================================================ IX. DEPLOYMENT
heading("IX", "From Benchmark to Factory: Visual Quality Control Deployment")

para(
    "A production deployment wraps the trained autoencoder in five stages. First, image acquisition: a fixed "
    "camera photographs each part under controlled lighting, replacing the curated Fashion-MNIST framing with "
    "real factory imagery. Second, preprocessing: resize to the model input size, normalize to [0, 1], and "
    "apply exactly the transforms used in training\u2014any mismatch here silently degrades the scores. "
    "Third, batched inference: the encoder\u2013decoder forward pass, which our measurements put at 0.21 ms "
    "per image on a two-core CPU (Table VIII). Fourth, scoring and thresholding: compute s(x) and compare "
    "against \u03c4, calibrated on a few thousand known-good parts from the line rather than on a validation "
    "split. Fifth, alerting: images above threshold enter an operator review queue, displayed input-versus-"
    "reconstruction exactly as in Figs. 8, 11, and 12, so the score arrives with a visual explanation."
)
para(
    "The latency arithmetic is favorable. At 0.21 ms per image, a single modest CPU scores roughly 4,700 "
    "images per second; a line inspecting 60 parts per minute consumes about 0.02% of that budget. Even "
    "allowing a tenfold slowdown for larger input resolutions, deeper backbones, and I/O overhead, CPU "
    "inference suffices and no GPU is required at the edge\u2014a meaningful simplification for cost, "
    "power, and maintenance. Batching parts (as in our batch-64 timing) amortizes framework overhead "
    "further, though single-image latency is already far below typical inter-part intervals."
)
para(
    "Calibration and drift deserve more care than compute. The 95th-percentile rule transfers directly from "
    "our protocol: collect several thousand known-good images from the line, compute their reconstruction "
    "errors with the deployed model, and set \u03c4 at the desired false-positive quantile. Because lighting, "
    "suppliers, and product revisions drift over weeks, \u03c4 must be re-estimated periodically, and the "
    "flagged rate itself becomes a monitoring signal\u2014a sudden rise means either process drift or a "
    "genuine defect burst, and both merit investigation. Keeping a small rolling buffer of recent normal "
    "images for recalibration is cheap insurance."
)
para(
    "The operating point is a business parameter, not a modeling constant. At our 95th-percentile setting "
    "with 10% anomaly prevalence, precision near 0.40 means roughly six of ten operator reviews are false "
    "alarms\u2014acceptable for triage, expensive at scale. On a real line where defects occur once in ten "
    "thousand parts, the same ranker needs a far stricter \u03c4, and the right tool for choosing it is the "
    "ROC curve (Fig. 6), not the F1 at an arbitrary threshold. Where product lines differ strongly, per-SKU "
    "models (one autoencoder per product) outperform a single global model, because each learns a tighter "
    "normal manifold; our single-model study is therefore the conservative case, and specialization can only "
    "help."
)
para(
    "Finally, the deployment creates its own training data. Every operator verdict\u2014defect or false "
    "alarm\u2014accumulates into a labeled set that did not exist before. Over months this enables the "
    "standard industrial hybrid: a supervised classifier trained on the accumulated labels handles the known "
    "defect modes with high precision, while the autoencoder remains the first line of defense for novel "
    "failure modes the classifier has never seen. The unsupervised system thus bootstraps the supervised one "
    "instead of competing with it [35]."
)
para(
    "Maintenance is scheduled, not reactive. The pipeline logs the score distribution\u2014mean, variance, "
    "and the fraction above \u03c4\u2014every shift, and a weekly job compares the current week against the "
    "validation baseline with a two-sample Kolmogorov\u2013Smirnov test. Sustained drift past the 1% "
    "significance level triggers an investigation: it may be dust on the lens, a new lighting rig, or a "
    "genuine change in the product mix, each with a different remedy (cleaning, recalibration, or "
    "retraining). The model itself is retrained quarterly on accumulated normal images, or immediately if "
    "the false-alarm rate doubles for two consecutive weeks. Because training costs roughly ten minutes on "
    "commodity hardware, the retraining budget is measured in operator attention, not compute\u2014which is "
    "why the honest reporting of Section XI matters: a team that knows its model's real precision plans its "
    "review staffing correctly."
)

# ================================================================ X. DOMAINS
heading("X", "Related Application Domains")

para(
    "The machinery of Sections II\u2013VIII\u2014learn normality, score by reconstruction error, threshold on "
    "normal data\u2014transfers well beyond clothing images. In medical imaging, Schlegl et al. [9] trained "
    "generative models on healthy retinal OCT scans and flagged pathologies as anomalies, using the same "
    "principle that healthy anatomy reconstructs well and lesions do not. The stakes sharpen the calibration "
    "problem: a false negative is a missed diagnosis, so the operating point moves far toward recall, and "
    "uncertainty quantification around s(x) becomes as important as the score itself."
)
para(
    "In industrial telemetry, Sakurada and Yairi [17] applied autoencoders to spacecraft sensor streams, "
    "treating sliding windows of multivariate readings exactly as we treat images: normal operating regimes "
    "compress cleanly, and anomalous regimes do not. Our image protocol maps onto this setting with only the "
    "front end changed\u2014one-dimensional convolutions over time instead of two-dimensional convolutions "
    "over space\u2014which suggests the architectural comparison of Section VI would largely carry over. "
    "Network intrusion detection follows the same pattern, with packet-flow features in place of pixels."
)
para(
    "Financial fraud detection applies the same one-class logic to tabular data: legitimate transactions are "
    "plentiful and fraud is rare, adaptive, and deliberately disguised. Dense autoencoders over engineered "
    "transaction features serve as the baseline there, and the surveys by Chalapathy and Chawla [32] and Pang "
    "et al. [33] document the migration of these methods from shallow models to deep ones across all three "
    "domains. Video surveillance adds a temporal dimension\u2014normal crowd motion reconstructs, abnormal "
    "events do not\u2014and predictive maintenance closes the loop back to Section IX, where vibration and "
    "temperature traces join images as inspection signals."
)
para(
    "Two of these domains merit a closer look because they extend the method along axes our study fixes. "
    "Video anomaly detection generalizes the input from a single frame to a spatiotemporal volume: "
    "convolutional LSTMs and 3D convolutions reconstruct short clips of normal activity (pedestrians walking, "
    "machines cycling), and events that violate the learned dynamics\u2014a person running through a "
    "walkway, a tool falling onto a conveyor\u2014produce large prediction or reconstruction errors. Our "
    "finding that convolutional inductive bias dominates dense layers (Section VI) predicts, correctly per "
    "the literature, that the spatiotemporal extension should win by an even larger margin, since the "
    "temporal structure is exactly what dense layers cannot exploit. Predictive maintenance is the industrial "
    "cousin: the autoencoder ingests multivariate sensor traces from healthy equipment, and rising "
    "reconstruction error becomes an early-warning signal weeks before failure. Here the deployment "
    "machinery of Section IX\u2014threshold calibration, drift monitoring, operator review\u2014is not an "
    "add-on but the product itself, because a false alarm stops a production line and a missed signal costs "
    "a machine."
)
para(
    "Ruff et al. [34] unify these applications under a single principle: deep one-class methods learn a "
    "compact description of normality and score deviations from it, whether the deviation is a sandal among "
    "sneakers, a lesion among healthy retinas, or an intrusion among benign flows. Our study is a controlled "
    "instance of that principle with the experimental variables\u2014architecture, regularization, "
    "threshold\u2014isolated one at a time, which is precisely what makes the findings portable: the BN "
    "ablation of Section VII, for example, warns any of these domains that normalization is load-bearing, not "
    "cosmetic."
)

# ================================================================ XI. LIMITS
heading("XI", "Limitations and Threats to Validity")
para(
    "Several limitations qualify these findings. First, every number reported comes from a single random seed "
    "(42). Deep networks exhibit run-to-run variance, and while our effect sizes\u2014particularly the ablation "
    "gaps\u2014are large enough to be credible, proper confidence intervals would require repeated runs that our "
    "compute budget did not allow. Second, we train on a 10,000-image subset for only 8 epochs on a two-core "
    "CPU. This keeps the study reproducible on modest hardware, which we consider a feature, but it also means "
    "the models are undertrained relative to what the architectures could achieve with more data and time; the "
    "absolute metric values should be read as lower bounds, not ceilings."
)
para(
    "Third, the anomaly task uses one fixed, visually distinctive class. Sandals differ from every normal class "
    "in silhouette, so our protocol measures coarse out-of-distribution detection rather than the fine-grained "
    "defect spotting a factory system needs\u2014a scuffed sneaker among pristine sneakers would be far harder. "
    "Fourth, mean squared error is a crude perceptual metric: it penalizes a one-pixel shift as harshly as a "
    "structural defect and ignores the texture statistics humans actually notice. Alternatives such as SSIM or "
    "perceptual losses would likely change both reconstructions and rankings. Fifth, the 32-dimensional latent "
    "code was chosen by convention, not by search; a smaller bottleneck might sharpen anomaly separation by "
    "forcing harsher compression, or a larger one might dilute it. Finally, Fashion-MNIST is a clean academic "
    "dataset, and none of these results have been validated on real industrial imagery with genuine defects."
)
para(
    "A further limitation concerns the evaluation protocol itself. The 95th-percentile threshold is principled "
    "but arbitrary: it fixes the operating point before seeing any anomalies, which is the honest choice, yet "
    "a different application might tolerate a 1% or 20% false-positive rate, and our reported precision, "
    "recall, and F1 would all shift accordingly. More importantly, the test set\u2019s 10% anomaly prevalence is "
    "generous compared with real inspection lines, where defects may occur once in ten thousand items. "
    "Detection difficulty scales with prevalence for any thresholded score, so our metrics describe ranking "
    "ability in a balanced testbed, not expected performance on a factory floor. The threshold-free ROC-AUC "
    "transfers better, but even it says nothing about how to pick \u03c4 when anomalies are too rare to calibrate on."
)
para(
    "On the modeling side, two choices were fixed by convention rather than evidence. The 32-dimensional latent "
    "code was not selected by search: a narrower bottleneck might sharpen anomaly separation by forcing harsher "
    "compression of normal structure, while a wider one might memorize enough texture to reconstruct sandals "
    "too well and erase the signal entirely. Likewise, the denoising noise level \u03c3=0.3 was set a priori; we "
    "report no sensitivity analysis over noise levels, and a much weaker or stronger corruption could change the "
    "denoising model\u2019s standing relative to the plain Conv-AE. Dropout appears only in the FC-AE at a single "
    "rate of 0.1, so its interaction with batch normalization\u2014and whether convolutional models would benefit "
    "from it\u2014remains untested."
)
para(
    "Finally, the study compares three autoencoders against each other but against no outside baseline: no PCA "
    "reconstruction, no one-class SVM on raw pixels or on our latent codes, and no variational or GAN-based "
    "detector. Our claims are therefore strictly comparative within the autoencoder family, not statements "
    "about the state of anomaly detection on Fashion-MNIST. The compute budget is also worth stating plainly: "
    "everything ran on a shared two-core CPU, which constrained us to a single seed, a 10,000-image training "
    "subset, and short epoch budgets. The upside is genuine reproducibility for readers with modest hardware; "
    "the downside is that every absolute number in this paper is a lower bound, and any of these limitations "
    "is a legitimate reason to extend the work."
)

subheading("F", "Threats to Validity")
para(
    "Beyond scope limitations, several factors could threaten the conclusions themselves. On internal "
    "validity, the ranking Conv-AE > FC-AE rests on a single seed. The batch-normalization ablation gap "
    "(MSE 0.01979 \u2192 0.03767) is large enough to survive plausible run-to-run variance, but the dropout "
    "gap (0.01979 \u2192 0.02144) is small and could shrink or reverse across seeds\u2014we report it as "
    "suggestive rather than conclusive. The 4-epoch ablation budget is a second confound: batch normalization "
    "accelerates optimization, so part of its advantage may reflect faster convergence rather than a better "
    "final optimum, and equalizing on fully converged models could narrow the gap."
)
para(
    "On construct validity, MSE is the construct standing in for visual abnormality, and it is an imperfect "
    "one: a one-pixel shift scores like a structural defect, while a subtle texture change humans would flag "
    "may barely register. Our claims about which architecture is better at anomaly detection are therefore "
    "claims about MSE-ranked detection specifically. Class choice is a second construct threat: Sandal is a "
    "visually distinctive and relatively easy anomaly, and a harder held-out class\u2014say, Shirt among other "
    "tops\u2014could compress or even reorder the ROC-AUC gaps, or change the standing of the denoising "
    "model relative to the plain Conv-AE."
)
para(
    "On external validity, Fashion-MNIST is clean, centered, and low-resolution, while real inspection imagery "
    "has clutter, variable lighting, and defects far subtler than an entirely different garment category. "
    "Until this protocol is replicated on a dataset like MVTec AD [35], our numbers describe the method on a "
    "benchmark rather than expected factory performance. We state this plainly because the deployment "
    "discussion of Section IX is only as credible as the gap between benchmark and factory is honestly "
    "acknowledged."
)

# ================================================================ XII. CONCLUSION
heading("XII", "Conclusion and Future Work")
para(
    "We compared fully connected, convolutional, and denoising autoencoders on Fashion-MNIST under a strict "
    "one-class protocol with the Sandal class held out of training. The convolutional autoencoder performed "
    "best, reaching an ROC-AUC of 0.857 with the lowest reconstruction error (MSE 0.01459) while using roughly "
    "a quarter of the parameters of the dense baseline\u2014evidence that convolutional weight sharing is as "
    "valuable for reconstruction as it is for recognition. The denoising variant traded a small clean-image "
    "penalty for robustness to input corruption. Threshold-dependent metrics were modest at our conservative "
    "95th-percentile operating point, but the threshold-free metrics confirm genuine separability between "
    "normal and anomalous images. Our ablations showed batch normalization to be essential\u2014its removal "
    "nearly doubled error and sharply reduced detection quality\u2014while dropout contributed smaller, "
    "consistent gains."
)
para(
    "Future work should extend the protocol in four directions. First, multi-class and fine-grained anomaly "
    "settings\u2014holding out several classes, or synthesizing subtle defects within a class\u2014would better "
    "approximate industrial inspection. Second, replacing MSE with structural or perceptual losses may improve "
    "both reconstruction fidelity and the alignment between the anomaly score and human notions of defect. "
    "Third, a direct comparison with variational autoencoders and GAN-based detectors under the same fixed "
    "protocol would situate reconstruction-error methods within the broader anomaly detection landscape. "
    "Fourth, validating the pipeline on real defect imagery is the necessary step from benchmark to "
    "deployment. All code, trained weights, and figure-generation scripts are structured for reproducibility "
    "so that each of these extensions can build directly on this work."
)
para(
    "Several further directions follow directly from the new material in this paper. The sensitivity analyses "
    "we did not run\u2014over the noise level \u03c3, the latent dimension d, and the random seed\u2014are the "
    "cheapest next experiments and would convert our single-point estimates into proper ablations. Replacing "
    "MSE with the structural similarity index [31] or a perceptual loss would test whether the rankings of "
    "Section VI survive a metric closer to human vision. A head-to-head comparison with variational "
    "autoencoders [4], [23] and GAN-based detectors [37], [38] under the same fixed protocol would situate "
    "reconstruction-error methods within the modern landscape, and replicating the protocol on MVTec AD [35] "
    "would test the external-validity claims of Section XI directly. Finally, an operator-in-the-loop study "
    "measuring time-to-detection and false-alarm burden, rather than AUC alone, is the evaluation that "
    "ultimately matters for the deployment sketched in Section IX."
)
para(
    "Stepping back, the broader significance of this work is methodological rather than algorithmic. "
    "Anomaly detection papers routinely report a new architecture beating a baseline whose training details "
    "are underspecified; our study inverts that pattern by making the baseline itself the object of study, "
    "documented down to the epoch. The payoff is a set of portable, quantitative facts\u2014convolutions beat "
    "dense layers at one-quarter the parameters, batch normalization is load-bearing, the operating point "
    "matters more than the architecture ranking it preserves\u2014that any practitioner can use without "
    "adopting our models. If the field produced more such studies, the gap between benchmark numbers and "
    "deployed performance would narrow, because the numbers that survive honest reporting are the ones worth "
    "building on."
)

heading("", "Acknowledgment")
para(
    "The authors thank the course faculty for the project framework, the one-class evaluation protocol, and "
    "the plagiarism and formatting checks that improved this manuscript."
)

heading("Appendix A", "Epoch-by-Epoch Training Log")
para(
    "Table XII reproduces the per-epoch training and validation MSE from the run log for the three baseline "
    "models, so that the loss curves of Fig. 1 can be checked numerically. Two dynamics are visible in the "
    "numbers. First, the convolutional models start worse than the fully connected baseline\u2014epoch-1 "
    "validation MSE is 0.04130 (Conv) and 0.04470 (Denoising) against 0.03093 (FC)\u2014but overtake it by "
    "epoch 4 and never look back, ending at 0.01468 and 0.01735 against 0.01996. The crossover reflects the "
    "inductive bias at work: convolutions must first learn useful filters before weight sharing pays off, "
    "whereas dense layers fit quickly and then plateau. Second, validation loss tracks training loss "
    "throughout for all three models, with no divergence that would indicate overfitting\u2014expected on "
    "only 8 epochs, and consistent with the mild dropout effect measured in Section VII. The denoising "
    "model trails the plain Conv-AE by a near-constant 0.002\u20130.003 at every epoch, the steady price of "
    "its noise-robustness objective."
)
